{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a persistent rise, add a positive level shift to the whole path (model is systematically too low by ~+3 to +10 €/t, with the gap generally widening beyond ~day 7).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: apply a larger uplift for mid-horizon forecasts (≈days 8–25) than for days 1–5; the model’s slope is too flat versus realized outcomes in these examples.",
    "Regime-change lag: after a breakout to new highs (recent prices > prior 20–30d range), assume the model lags the new regime—raise the forecast baseline rather than expecting near-term mean reversion.",
    "Avoid false mean reversion: if the model projects a drift back toward ~70–75 while realized prices are making higher highs, damp/disable mean-reversion components and keep forecasts closer to the recent high/upper channel.",
    "Late-horizon drawdown overshoot: when the model predicts a sharp late drop (e.g., falling into the high-60s within 20–30d) while the market is in a strong uptrend, cap the magnitude of the decline (reduce negative slope) unless recent data already show a confirmed reversal."
  ]
}
